course: chinese-street
lesson: 15
title: School words
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Ming goes to two schools: the normal one all week, and Zhōngwén xuéxiào (Chinese school) on Saturday morning, where he learns to read and write. You only ever hear the words, so you learn them by ear from him.
explain: At Chinese school the teacher walks in, everyone stands up and sings out together, slowly, «lǎoshī hǎo!» (hello, teacher), and the teacher answers «tóngxuémen hǎo» (hello, class). It sounds like a little choir.
explain: shàng kè is lessons starting and xià kè is lessons over: shàng is «up», xià is «down», like the bell lifting school up and dropping it again. At xià kè everyone runs out.
explain: The words you'll need most when your Chinese runs out are three: tīng bù dǒng (I hear you but don't understand), wǒ bù zhīdào (I don't know) and wǒ wàng le (I forgot) — careful, wàng falls; rising wáng is a king.
fact: lǎoshī («LAOW-shr») means teacher; children say lǎoshī or surname plus lǎoshī, like Wáng lǎoshī, and never a teacher's first name, rather like «Miss» in Britain.
fact: lǎoshī hǎo («LAOW-shr HOW») is the whole class standing and greeting the teacher in a slow chorus; the teacher replies tóngxuémen hǎo, hello class.
fact: tóngxué («TONG-shweh», both rising) means classmate, a little more formal than péngyou (friend); a classmate is not always a friend.
fact: shàng kè («SHAHNG KUH») means lessons start or to be in class; shàng kè le! means the bell has gone; shàng xué is to go to school.
fact: xià kè («SHYAH KUH») means the lesson is over, break time; shàng (up) and xià (down) often mark start and end.
fact: zuòyè («DZWAW-YEH») means homework; parents ask zuòyè zuò wán le ma? (finished your homework?), and many Chinese families are strict about homework before play.
fact: kǎoshì («KAOW-SHR») means a test or exam; kǎo de zěnmeyàng? (how did the test go?) is the first thing many Chinese parents ask.
fact: yìbǎi fēn («ee-BYE FUN») means a hundred marks, full marks; fēn also means minute and penny.
fact: Zhōngwén xuéxiào («JONG-wun SHWEH-shyow») is Chinese school, at the weekend; many British Chinese children go to learn reading and writing, so it can feel like school six days a week.
fact: tīng bù dǒng («TING boo DONG») means I hear it but don't understand, fine to say to the friend's family; kàn bù dǒng is the same for reading.
fact: wǒ bù zhīdào («waw boo JR-dow») means I don't know; to a teacher, adding duìbuqǐ makes it softer.
fact: wǒ wàng le («waw WAHNG luh») means I forgot; wàng (forget) falls, while rising wáng is king and the surname Wang.
exercise: What do children at Ming's Chinese school call their teacher? | lǎoshī, or surname plus lǎoshī | The teacher's first name, like at home | tóngxué, the polite word | āyí
exercise: The Chinese school teacher walks in. What does the class say? | lǎoshī hǎo, standing up together | wǒ wàng le, sitting down quietly | xià kè, running out | zàijiàn, waving at the teacher
exercise: How does the teacher answer lǎoshī hǎo? | tóngxuémen hǎo | lǎoshī hǎo | wǒ bù zhīdào | shàng kè le
exercise: What does tóngxué mean? | Classmate | Best friend | Class teacher | Homework book
exercise: «shàng kè le!» What has happened? | The bell's gone and lessons are starting | School is over for the day, so everyone goes home | There is a big test today | The teacher is ill
exercise: What does xià kè mean? | The lesson's over, break time | Lessons are starting now | Sit down at your desks | Line up
exercise: shàng and xià in school words mean | Up and down, start and end | Left and right, this way and that | Fast and slow, early and late | Teacher and pupil, old and young
exercise: Ming's mum asks «zuòyè zuò wán le ma?». What is she asking? | Have you finished your homework? | Did you eat your lunch at school? | Where is your bag? | Who is your new teacher this year?
exercise: What is a kǎoshì? | A test or exam | A school bag | A break | A classmate
exercise: Ming got yìbǎi fēn. How did he do? | Full marks, a hundred | He failed | He was a hundred minutes late | He lost a penny
exercise: What else can fēn mean? | Minute and penny | Teacher and pupil | Hot and cold | Up and down
exercise: What is Zhōngwén xuéxiào? | Chinese school at the weekend, for reading and writing | A Chinese restaurant near the school gates | The Chinese word for break | A school in China only, never in Britain or anywhere else
exercise: Ming's granny talks fast and you're lost. What can you say? | tīng bù dǒng | kàn bù dǒng | yìbǎi fēn | shàng kè
exercise: Which phrase is «I can't understand it» for reading? | kàn bù dǒng | tīng bù dǒng | wǒ wàng le | xià kè
exercise: The teacher asks you something and you have no idea. What do you say? | wǒ bù zhīdào | wǒ wàng le | lǎoshī hǎo | tóngxué
exercise: You left your homework at home. What do you say? | wǒ wàng le, I forgot | wǒ bù zhīdào, I don't know | tīng bù dǒng, I can't follow | yìbǎi fēn, full marks
exercise: Why must wàng in wǒ wàng le fall? | Rising wáng means king, the surname Wang | Falling makes the whole thing a question | Rising wáng means homework | It doesn't matter at all
test: «my mate says chinese kids call teachers by first name like Mr Dave lol» Is he right? | No, they say lǎoshī or surname plus lǎoshī | Yes, always the first name, it's friendlier | Yes, but only at the weekend | No, they call all teachers āyí
test: «ming is my tóngxué so he's def my best friend» Is that right? | Not necessarily: tóngxué is classmate, not friend | Yes, tóngxué means best friend for life, 100 per cent | Yes, tóngxué means brother | No, tóngxué means teacher
test: What would happen if you said «wǒ wáng le» with a rising tone? | It would sound like «I king» instead of «I forgot» | Nothing, both tones mean forget, it's the same word | It would mean I don't know | It would mean I'm late
test: Ming's mum, as soon as he's home: «kǎo de zěnmeyàng?». What does she want to know? | How the test went | What he ate for lunch | Where his coat and bag are | Who he played with
test: «ugh, can't come out, zuòyè first, mum's strict» What's going on? | His family wants homework done before play | He's ill in bed and can't go out, poor thing | He's at Chinese school | He's going to bed early tonight, at six
test: «ming's moody every saturday, what's his problem» What's a likely reason? | He has Chinese school, like six days of school a week | Saturday is a test day in every single British school | He has no homework | Saturday is unlucky in China
test: In a game a pupil runs in shouting «xià kè!» as the bell rings for the first lesson. What should he have shouted? | shàng kè le! | wǒ wàng le! | yìbǎi fēn! | lǎoshī hǎo, sitting down!
test: You say «wǒ bù zhīdào» to the teacher with a shrug and nothing else. How can you make it softer? | Add duìbuqǐ | Shout it louder | Say it twice fast | Say xià kè after it
test: «is fēn like marks? cos the bus driver said wǔ fēn» What did the driver probably mean? | Five minutes or five pence: fēn has several meanings | Five marks out of a hundred, for showing your ticket | Five teachers | Five classmates on the bus
test: «oi new kid, tīng dǒng ma? you deaf or what» You're lost in the fast talk. What's an honest answer? | tīng bù dǒng | yìbǎi fēn | xià kè | lǎoshī hǎo
test: A cartoon shows the class greeting the teacher by sitting and mumbling. What does a real Chinese class do? | Stand up and sing out lǎoshī hǎo together | Clap three times and sit down | Say zàijiàn | Stay silent and bow very low, one by one
exam: Identify the term by which pupils customarily address an instructor in a Chinese school. | lǎoshī | tóngxué | āyí | shūshu
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the expressions shàng kè and xià kè. | They denote the commencement and the conclusion of a lesson | They denote, respectively, the morning and the afternoon session | They denote a test and its result | They denote, respectively, a teacher and a pupil
exam: Ascertain the expression whereby a pupil indicates that spoken words have been heard but not comprehended. | tīng bù dǒng | kàn bù dǒng | wǒ wàng le | yìbǎi fēn
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the expression yìbǎi fēn? | It denotes a perfect score of one hundred marks | It denotes a period of one hundred minutes of homework | It denotes one hundred pupils in a class | It denotes the sum of one hundred pounds
